# Devstral 24B dense -- the first arm chosen for what it was TRAINED to do
# rather than for what fits.
#
# WHY THIS MODEL. Of the local models under the memory ceiling, it is the only
# one that both lists `tools` in its Ollama capabilities and publishes an
# agentic benchmark (46.8% SWE-Bench Verified). The two obvious smaller
# candidates -- deepseek-coder-v2:16b (8.9 GB, MoE) and qwen2.5-coder:14b
# (9.0 GB) -- were rejected on 2026-09-13 for the same reason: no `tools`
# capability. opencode drives every edit through a function call, so such a
# model produces a fast row with zero diff lines, which in the published table
# is indistinguishable from a model that tried and failed. Checked before
# pulling, not after a wasted leg.
#
# MEMORY. 14 GB of weights, dense. gpt-oss-20b runs here at 13 GB; every e08
# leg dies at 18 GB. This sits between them, so it is also a measurement of
# where the wall actually is on this machine.
#
# NOT COMPARABLE TO EXISTING ROWS. cfg_model is a campaign constant (chapter
# 17): devstral rows pool with no other engine's. Every project to be compared
# has to be re-run under this config.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. gpt-oss-20b scores 0.9020 on p02 under m48_syntax_gate. The
# proposition is that a model trained for multi-file tool-driven editing beats
# a general model of similar size on the larger projects, where the 20B's
# advantage should decay. p02 first; p03 only if p02 justifies the night.

ENGINE=opencode
MODEL=litellm/devstral-24b
# MODEL: provider/model. `devstral-24b` is the gateway name for the Ollama tag
# devstral:24b, pulled as published -- no Modelfile, so nothing to build.
PROVIDER=
BASE_URL=
EFFORT=medium
MAX_TURNS=60
MAX_BUDGET_USD=4.00
REPEATS=1
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=

REVIEW_PASS=none
REVIEW_MAX_BUDGET_USD=1.00
REVIEW_MODEL=
REVIEW_CMD=
REVIEW_PROMPT=
REVIEW_WEIGHT=1
REVIEW_FEEDBACK=0
FIX_MODEL=
BEST_OF_N=1
